    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've got a heifer here that just came out of a rumenotomy about 6 hours ago. Can you give me a step-by-step protocol for what I need to be watching for over the next few days, specifically regarding the incision site and diet? Also, what are the main signs of internal infection I should look out for? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "After a rumenotomy, look out for redness, swelling, or unusual discharge from the incision site as well as elevated heart and respiration rates, decreased appetite, or abnormal feces in your heifer as signs of infection.\n\nOver the next few days, it's important to:\n\n1. Keep the incision site clean and dry, regularly checking for any changes. Cover it gently with gauze to prevent flies and dirt.\n2. Administer the prescribed antibiotics and pain medication as the vet directed, and make sure he’s drinking enough water.\n3. Start reintroducing his usual diet slowly. He may be a bit sensitive to ingesta after surgery, so a forage-first or high-fiber diet can help with digestion and allow him to eat.\n4. Keep an eye out for signs of colic, bloat, fever, or behavioral changes.\n\nIf you suspect internal infection, behaviors can include loss of appetite, lethargy, faster breathing, and pus or abnormal feces. Contact your vet if these symptoms arise or if the injury doesn't begin to seem to heal. Try to avoid rough handling and stress.",
      "source": "target",
