    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm a bit worried about the internal stuff though, like if there's something going on inside the rumen that i can't see yet. what are the subtle signs that a serious infection or abscess might be developing deep inside before it actually shows up on the outside? like is there a specific way they act or smell that might tip me off early?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your concern. Internal abscesses or a serious infection in the rumen can be tricky to spot without A/V endoscopy. Here are some subtle signs that may suggest complications if they develop:\n\n1. Lethargy or a change in behavior indicating discomfort that may affect their normal activity patterns.\n2. Pedal withdrawal or reluctance to ambulate, which could be a reaction to internal pain.\n3. A change in manure—for darker, more watery types—can be an early sign of ruminal disorders.\n4. Repeated instances of bloating and gas that don’t resolve could be signs of a deeper internal issue.\n\nIf you are ever unsure or notice these changes, contact your veterinarian. They can schedule internal examinations to ensure problems are caught quickly when they’re easier to manage. In the meantime, please follow the care instructions provided by your vet.",
      "source": "target",
